= Introduction and related work

Radar tracking requires inferring position and velocity from noisy observations. The Kalman filter provides a recursive linear minimum-mean-square-error estimator when its model and second-order statistics are correctly specified; in the linear Gaussian setting it also recovers the exact conditional mean @kalman1960. Radar range and bearing are nonlinear functions of Cartesian position, so a linear state model alone does not make the observation problem linear.

Two common responses are to linearize the observation map or approximate its transformed moments. Julier and Uhlmann explain the unscented approach and its relationship to local linearization @julier2004. Arasaratnam and Haykin introduce a spherical-radial cubature rule and a square-root implementation @arasaratnam2009. A different route converts radar observations into Cartesian coordinates before a linear update. The work of Lerro and Bar-Shalom @lerro1993 and the later study by Spitzmiller and Adhami @spitzmiller2010 motivate careful treatment of conversion bias and covariance. Pei gives a recent comparison of converted-measurement methods @pei2025.

This course project adapts the maneuvering-ship scenario in Section 6.1 of Arasaratnam's thesis @arasaratnam-thesis2009. It adds a constant-velocity converted-measurement baseline and four noise sensitivity sweeps. The contribution is an experimental comparison, not a new filtering algorithm or an exact reproduction of the thesis. In particular, the baseline does not use the turning model available to the nonlinear filters; this structural difference is part of the comparison.
